SOME THINGS YOU SHOULD KNOW
================================================================================

The T-RIX approach by FGW published in 2023 found that the best correlation between RIX as a predictor of complexity
and the Complexity according to the survey conducted, was at 0.033. This threshold value is an "Experience based deduction".

windPRO allows users to calculate T-RIX  with a threshold of 0.033.
Sectorwise RIX calculations can only be carried out with a minimum threshold of 0.0501, which is close enough to the 0.033 value.

However, this value translates to a slope angle of roughly 3 degrees.
A 3° terrain slope is entirely within the operational envelope of WAsP, and as a result,
RIX_0.0501 didn't seem to translate to an uncertainty predictor inside the model.

WAsP PERFORMANCE ENVELOPE

The operating envelope of WAsP cannot be defined quantitatively for all
likely situations, but it is dominated by the main considerations listed
below.

Accurate predictions using the WAsP package may be obtained provided that
both the reference and predicted sites are clearly:

a. Subject to the same weather regime
   - The maximum separation between sites is best defined by the typical
     scale of prevailing synoptic weather systems.

b. Prevailing weather conditions are close to being neutrally stable
   - Atmospheric effects are complex and no correction is available for
     non-neutrally stable conditions.
   - Unstable conditions are expected to increase orographic prediction error.
   - Stable conditions may significantly decrease the error.
   - Coastal sites may be dominated by sea-breeze systems that do not reach
     inland locations.

c. The surrounding terrain is not too steep
   - Orographic prediction errors are likely when surrounding slopes exceed 0.3.
   - A slope of 0.3 is generally accepted as the upper limit to ensure
     predominantly attached flow.
   - Since prediction error depends on differences in site ruggedness,
     accurate results may still be possible between two rugged but similar sites.
   - Large-scale terrain effects such as channelling must be absent.
   - Large differences in elevation should be treated with care.

d. High quality data
   - Measured wind speed data at the reference site must be of high quality.
   - Preliminary data processing and sector sorting must be reliable.

e. Proper use of the WAsP program
   - Great care is required when operating outside the recommended limits.
   - For rugged terrain, an orographic performance indicator has been
     developed to improve interpretation of such predictions.

Reference: Risø-R-995(EN)

