1. MODEL OVERVIEW
================================================================================

Two Bayesian uncertainty models have been developed:

MODEL 1: Energy Yield (EY) Uncertainty Model (v6)
-------------------------------------------------
Target: Energy yield deviation between cross-prediction and actual measurement
Formula: EY_deviation = (EY_cross_predicted - EY_actual) / EY_actual
Aggregation: Energy-weighted across 12 sectors

MODEL 2: Windspeed (WS) Uncertainty Model (v2)
----------------------------------------------
Target: Relative windspeed deviation between cross-prediction and actual
Formula: WS_deviation = |WS_predicted - WS_actual| / max(WS_actual, 3.0)
Aggregation: Energy-weighted across 12 sectors (using Energy sector-wise weights of cross prediction, i.e, weight_energy_predicted)

Both models have the same structure:
- Mean model (μ): Predicts the expected deviation
- Sigma model (σ): Predicts the uncertainty/spread of the deviation

Mean Model:
    μ = α + pair_offset + β_dz × dz

    Where:
    - α = global intercept
    - pair_offset = random effect for each undirected pair
    - β_dz = effect of height difference on deviation

Sigma Model:
    log(σ) = log_σ₀ + Σ(γᵢ × zᵢ)

    Where:
    - log_σ₀ = baseline log-sigma
    - γᵢ = effect of each driver
    - zᵢ = standardized (z-scored) driver values

================================================================================
2. SIGMA DRIVERS (FEATURES INCLUDED IN MODEL)
================================================================================

Both models include 10 sigma drivers. Each driver increases uncertainty when
its value is high.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 1: Distance Score
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Column needed: distance_m, distance_A, distance_B
Formula: dist_score = max(distance_m - distance_A, 0) / (distance_B - distance_A)

Description:
    Measures how far apart the WTG and MM are, normalized by terrain-adjusted
    acceptable distance bounds. A score of 0 means the sites are very close
    (within the lower bound), while higher scores indicate greater separation.

Physical meaning:
    Wind conditions decorrelate over distance. The further apart two sites are,
    the less similar their wind climates will be, making cross-prediction
    less reliable.

Effect:
    EY v6: Rank #7 (1.18× per +1 SD)
    WS v2: Rank #1 (1.53× per +1 SD) ← DOMINANT for windspeed

Author's notes:
    In the old model for uncertainty, this used to be the only predictor of windspeed uncertainty.
    The linear increase of uncertainty with distance brought about the idea of coming up with a proper analysis.

From Similarity principle disproving the distance theory:

In this section, some of the more typical conditions are listed.
The list is not exhaustive — in each case, the user must determine
which conditions are most important for the specific application.

REGIONAL WIND CLIMATE
- Similar regional wind climates (number one criterion when using WAsP)
- Similar general atmospheric forcing effects
- Similar atmospheric stability conditions

OROGRAPHY
- Difference in RIX less than 10 (number one criterion in complex terrain)
- Similar slopes, exposure, and orientation of main topographical features
- Similar site elevation above sea level

ROUGHNESS
- Similar background roughness
- Equal orientation and distance to major roughness changes (e.g., coastlines)

OBSTACLES
- Same degree of "three-dimensionality"
- Similar obstacle orientation

OTHER SITE CHARACTERISTICS
- Similar height above ground level of predictor (anemometer)
  and predicted site (wind turbine hub height)

NOTES ON RIX

RIX (Ruggedness Index) is a coarse measure of flow separation.
It was proposed as an indicator of terrain complexity.

ΔRIX (Delta RIX) has been developed as an orographic performance indicator.
It can predict the sign and approximate magnitude of prediction error
when applying linear flow models such as WAsP in complex terrain.

"Note, that the horizontal distance between the predictor and the predicted sites does not appear on the list
above; one should much rather use similarity in regional wind climate."


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 2: |dz| (Absolute Height Difference)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Column needed: dz
Formula: |dz| = absolute value of (WTG_elevation - MM_elevation)

Description:
    The magnitude of elevation difference between the WTG and MM locations,
    regardless of which is higher. Measured in meters.

Physical meaning:
    Higher height means higher windspeed, rule of thumb is that 100 meters of elevation chnages windspeed by ~10%.
    This alone has been the biggest mean driver for the model.


Effect:
    EY v6: Rank #1 (1.43× per +1 SD) ← DOMINANT for energy yield
    WS v2: Rank #9 (1.12× per +1 SD) ← NOT SO DOMINANT, which is weird, still being looked into.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 3: |log_speedup| (Speedup Difference Magnitude)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Columns needed: overall_speedup_WTG_factor, overall_speedup_MM_factor
Formula: |log_speedup| = |log(speedup_WTG / speedup_MM)|

Description:
    The magnitude of the log-ratio between speedup factors at the WTG and MM
    locations. Speedup factors describe how terrain affects wind speed
    relative to a reference condition.

Physical meaning:
    Large differences in speedup factors indicate that the terrain affects
    the two locations very differently. This makes transferring wind
    predictions between sites more uncertain.

Note on speedup factors:
    A speedup factor > 1 means the terrain accelerates wind (e.g., hilltops)
    A speedup factor < 1 means the terrain decelerates wind (e.g., behind hills)

Why log?
    The speedup can vary on both sides of 1, that is < 1 or > 1.
    Moving to either side has a proportional impact on the uncertainty. And log helps me tackle this impact.

Effect:
    EY v6: Rank #2 (1.29× per +1 SD)
    WS v2: Rank #8 (1.12× per +1 SD)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 4: RIX_avg (So called Terrain Severity)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Column needed: RIX_avg_0.3_sector
Formula: Energy-weighted average across 12 sectors

Description:
    RIX (Ruggedness Index) measures terrain complexity. The 0.3 threshold
    identifies "severe" terrain where linear flow models may be unreliable.
    Flow separation is obviously outside the operational envelope of WAsP.
    RIX_avg is the average between WTG and MM locations.

Physical meaning:
    Complex terrain creates turbulent, non-linear wind flow that is harder
    to model. Higher RIX values indicate more challenging terrain for
    wind flow predictions.

Values:
    RIX = 0-5%: Generally flat or gentle terrain
    RIX = 5-15%: Moderately complex terrain
    RIX > 15%: Very complex terrain (wind flow models may be unreliable)

Effect:
    EY v6: Rank #6 (1.22× per +1 SD)
    WS v2: Rank #10 (1.12× per +1 SD)


Author's note:

I also tried with RIX_avg_0.0501_sector, but the results weren't encouraging.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 5: Severity Fraction - Artificial parameter created by Kshitij-
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Columns needed: dRIX_0.3_sector, dRIX_0.0501_sector
Formula: severity_fraction = |dRIX_0.3| / (|dRIX_0.3| + |dRIX_mild| + ε)
         where dRIX_mild = dRIX_0.0501 - dRIX_0.3

Description:
    Measures what fraction of the total terrain difference between WTG and MM
    is in "severe" terrain (RIX threshold 0.3) versus "mild" terrain.

Physical meaning:
    If terrain differences are concentrated in severe terrain (steep slopes,
    cliffs), the uncertainty is higher than if the same total difference
    were in milder terrain.

Values:
    0 = All terrain difference is in mild terrain
    1 = All terrain difference is in severe terrain

Effect:
    EY v6: Rank #4 (1.24× per +1 SD)
    WS v2: Rank #7 (1.14× per +1 SD)

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 6: Flip × Gradient (Deflection Effect) - Artificial parameter created by Kshitij-
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Columns needed: d_turning_deg, flip_fraction_weighted_by_predicted_power,
                overall_speedup_WTG_factor (for all 12 sectors)
Formula: flip_x_gradient = flip_fraction × |log(speedup_current) - log(speedup_adjacent)|

Description:
    Combines the "flip fraction" (energy borrowed from adjacent sectors due to
    wind deflection) with the speedup gradient between sectors. This captures
    cases where wind deflection causes energy to shift between sectors with
    very different speedup characteristics.

Physical meaning:
    When wind is deflected from one sector to another, and those sectors have
    very different terrain effects (speedup gradient), the prediction becomes
    more uncertain. The model can't accurately track where the energy "should"
    be attributed.

Effect:
    EY v6: Rank #5 (1.23× per +1 SD)
    WS v2: Rank #2 (1.25× per +1 SD)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 7: Sample Shortfall (Campaign Length)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Column needed: Sample_count_pred
Formula: sample_shortfall = log(max_samples_in_training) - log(pair_total_samples)

Description:
    Measures how much shorter the measurement campaign is compared to the
    longest campaign in the training data. Higher shortfall = shorter campaign.

Physical meaning:
    Shorter measurement campaigns capture less of the natural wind variability.
    The mean windspeed estimate is less stable, leading to higher uncertainty.

Note:
    This is "epistemic" uncertainty - it can be reduced by collecting more data.
    At deployment, if 1 year of data is available, sample_shortfall can be
    set to 0.

Effect:
    EY v6: Rank #3 (1.25× per +1 SD)
    WS v2: Rank #4 (1.17× per +1 SD)

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 8: Concentration Ratio (Energy Distribution) - Artificial parameter created by Kshitij-
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Column needed: weight_energy_predicted
Formula:
    effective_sectors = 1 / Σ(weight²)  [Inverse Herfindahl-Hirschman Index]
    concentration_ratio = (12 - effective_sectors) / 11

Description:
    Measures how concentrated the energy production is across sectors.
    If most energy comes from 2-3 sectors, the ratio is high. If energy
    is evenly distributed, the ratio is low.

Physical meaning:
    When energy is concentrated in few sectors, prediction errors in those
    sectors dominate the total. There's less "averaging out" of errors
    across directions.

Values:
    0 = Perfectly even distribution (1/12 = 8.3% per sector)
    1 = All energy from one sector
    Typical range: 0.2 - 0.8

Effect:
    EY v6: Rank #10 (1.15× per +1 SD)
    WS v2: Rank #5 (1.16× per +1 SD)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 9: Turning Std (Deflection Variability)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Column needed: d_turning_deg
Formula: turning_std = std(|d_turning_deg|) across 12 sectors

Description:
    Standard deviation of the absolute turning angle across all sectors.
    High values indicate that wind deflection varies greatly depending on
    wind direction.

Physical meaning:
    Variable deflection patterns indicate complex, direction-dependent terrain
    effects. The wind flow responds very differently depending on where it
    comes from, making predictions harder.

Effect:
    EY v6: Rank #8 (1.16× per +1 SD)
    WS v2: Rank #6 (1.15× per +1 SD)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DRIVER 10: Speedup Diff Std (Speedup Relationship Variability)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Columns needed: overall_speedup_WTG_factor, overall_speedup_MM_factor
Formula: speedup_diff_std = std(speedup_MM - speedup_WTG) across 12 sectors

Description:
    Standard deviation of the speedup difference (MM minus WTG) across all
    sectors. High values indicate an inconsistent relationship between the
    two sites.

Physical meaning:
    If the speedup difference varies greatly by direction, the terrain
    relationship between sites is complex. In some directions, WTG might
    be in faster wind; in others, MM might be faster. This inconsistency
    makes cross-prediction harder.

Note:
    If the difference changes sign across sectors (sometimes positive,
    sometimes negative), this indicates particularly complex terrain.

Effect:
    EY v6: Rank #9 (1.15× per +1 SD)
    WS v2: Rank #3 (1.17× per +1 SD)

