3. FEATURES EXPLORED BUT NOT INCLUDED
================================================================================

The following features were tested during model development but were not
included in the final models:

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FEATURE: Roughness Changes (roughness_ch_avg)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What it measures:
    Number of land cover transitions (e.g., forest→water) within 10km radius,
    averaged between WTG and MM locations.

Hypothesis:
    More roughness changes → internal boundary layers → harder to model

Exploration result:
    Paradoxical negative correlation at sector level (r = -0.08 with |EY|)
    Weak positive correlation at pair level (r = +0.26 with |EY|)
    Confounded with |dz| (r = +0.42)

Decision: NOT INCLUDED
    The signal was weak and in unexpected direction. May be capturing
    something else (e.g., flat terrain with many roughness changes vs
    complex terrain with few).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FEATURE: Windspeed Sensitivity (Power Curve Slope)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What it measures:
    Slope of the power curve at the mean windspeed (dP/dWS).
    Higher slope = small windspeed error → large power error.

Hypothesis:
    Steeper power curve regions should amplify windspeed errors into
    larger energy yield errors.

Exploration result:
    Negative correlation (r = -0.02 with |EY|) - opposite to hypothesis!

Explanation:
    High-slope regions (7-10 m/s) are actually the core operating range
    where the power curve is best calibrated. The negative correlation
    reflects model calibration quality, not physical amplification.

Decision: NOT INCLUDED
    The hypothesis was logical but empirically not supported.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FEATURE: dz × Distance Interaction
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What it measures:
    |dz| × distance_score

Hypothesis:
    Height errors might compound over distance. A 50m height difference
    at 500m distance is different from 50m at 5000m distance.

Exploration result:
    r = +0.16 with |EY|, r = +0.33 with |WS|

Decision: NOT INCLUDED
    Almost perfectly redundant with distance_score alone (r = 0.97).
    Distance already captures this effect.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FEATURE: Location Exclusions
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What it measures:
    Testing whether excluding specific "problematic" locations improves
    model fit.

    The idea here was: According to the EY deviation vs. TRIX graph, the clear outliers were Herzhausen, Ukhanda and Sallachy.
    So, an attempt to get better fit through ommission of these 3 projects was tried.

Locations tested:
    Sallachy (8 pairs), Herzhausen (3 pairs), Ukhanda (4 pairs)

Result:
    Excluding these locations made the model WORSE (R² dropped from 0.46 to 0.40).
    The "problematic" locations actually followed the physics better than average.

Decision: NOT IMPLEMENTED
    No locations are excluded from training data.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FEATURE: Reference Roughness Length
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What it measures:
    The "distant terrain roughness" used as basis for speedup calculations
    in WAsP.

Hypothesis:
    Might capture additional terrain information.

Assessment:
    Likely redundant with speedup factors, which already incorporate
    roughness effects in their calculation.

Decision: NOT EXPLORED
    Deferred due to likely redundancy.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FEATURE: Steep Region Exposure (7-9 m/s)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
What it measures:
    Fraction of energy coming from sectors where Mean_windspeed_predicted
    falls within the steep power curve region (7-9 m/s).

Hypothesis:
    Energy production in steep power curve regions should amplify windspeed
    errors into larger energy yield errors.

Exploration result:
    Negative correlation with |EY| - opposite to hypothesis.
    Tested multiple formulations: steep_fraction, weighted_proximity,
    distance_from_steep. All showed weak or negative correlations.

Explanation:
    Sites with ~8 m/s mean wind are often well-sited with good measurement
    campaigns. The amplification effect, if real, is likely dwarfed by
    terrain effects (|dz|, distance, speedup differences).

Decision: NOT INCLUDED
    Tested twice with different approaches. Effect not empirically supported.


